## What can a site owner control?

A site owner controls crawling through robots.txt user agents and through network rules. The operators in the table above document their own user agents, so use their pages for current names. Cloudflare describes robots.txt directives as "effectively a 'keep out' sign" and not formal access control ([Cloudflare Year in Review](https://blog.cloudflare.com/radar-2025-year-in-review/)).

OpenAI documents that each setting is independent. A site can allow OAI-SearchBot to appear in ChatGPT search and disallow GPTBot to signal that content should not be used for training:

```
User-agent: OAI-SearchBot
Allow: /

User-agent: GPTBot
Disallow: /
```

Vercel found that "The robots.txt file is effective for all measured crawlers" in its late 2024 test. Perplexity-User and ChatGPT-User are user-triggered fetchers, so robots.txt may not stop them, per their operators.

Cloudflare reported that fewer than 1% of sites on its network block search crawlers, while 17% block training (30 September 2026). My [robots.txt generator](/tools/robots-generator/) builds the file. My [AI visibility](/ai-visibility/) page explains how I check whether a site appears in AI answers.

## Methodology and limits

| Source | Publisher | Published | Sample and period | Type |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [Cloudflare Radar 2025 Year in Review](https://blog.cloudflare.com/radar-2025-year-in-review/) | Cloudflare | 15 December 2025 | Cloudflare network and customer base, 1 January to 2 December 2025 | Vendor network data |
| [The crawl-to-click gap](https://blog.cloudflare.com/crawlers-click-ai-bots-training/) | Cloudflare | 29 August 2025 | Cloudflare Radar data, January to July 2025 | Vendor network data |
| [The crawl before the fall of referrals](https://blog.cloudflare.com/ai-search-crawl-refer-ratio-on-radar/) | Cloudflare | 1 July 2025 | Radar metric launch, example week 19 to 26 June 2025 | Vendor network data |
| [The Internet has a second audience](https://blog.cloudflare.com/agentic-web/) | Cloudflare | 30 September 2026 | Cloudflare network, to September 2026 | Vendor network data |
| [The rise of the AI crawler](https://vercel.com/blog/the-rise-of-the-ai-crawler) | Vercel and MERJ | 17 December 2024 | Vercel network and test sites, one month (dates not stated) | Vendor network data |
| [66 billion bot requests analysis](https://www.hostinger.com/blog/ai-bot-analysis) | Hostinger | 20 January 2026 | 66.7 billion log entries, 5 million hosted sites, three 6-day windows | Vendor hosting data |

Limits that apply to every figure on this page:

- Each vendor measures its own traffic. Cloudflare, Vercel and Hostinger sell network, hosting or security products.
- None of the samples is a sample of the web, and none is UK-specific.
- Cloudflare's HTML shares and its Verified Bot shares have different bases. Hostinger's group shares are a third base.
- Crawl-to-refer ratios can overstate when referrals carry no Referer header. A ratio mixes training, search and user-triggered crawlers.
- I did not quote the Cloudflare Radar dashboard, which returned an access error to my fetch. Ahrefs publishes an AI bot activity benchmark whose values sit in interactive charts I could not read, so I left it out.
- Vercel's data is from late 2024 and describes one month on its network.
- I did not use figures from blogs that repeat these reports.

## How to cite this page

Cite the primary source for any figure you publish. A safe pattern:

> Cloudflare reported that AI bots made an average of 4.2% of HTML requests across its customer base in 2025 (Cloudflare Radar 2025 Year in Review, 15 December 2025).

To cite this summary: Sunny Patel, "AI crawler traffic statistics: how much bots take and send back", sunnypatel.co.uk/blog/ai-crawler-traffic-statistics/, last checked 24 November 2026.
